Algorithm: Contact Management System
Main

Step 1: START. Create an empty circular doubly linked list, an array of 10 free slots and an empty queue.

Step 2: Display the menu: Add, Delete, Search, Display forward, Display backward, Slot status, Undo delete, Delete history, Exit.

Step 3: Read the choice and call the matching function.

Step 4: Repeat Steps 2 and 3 until choice = 0. Free all nodes of the list. STOP.

Add Contact

Step 5: Find the first free slot in the array. If none is free, display “No slots available” and stop.

Step 6: Read contact ID. If the ID already exists in the list, stop.

Step 7: Read name, phone and email.

Step 8: Create a new node and insert it at the end of the list (link it between tail and head and update the prev/next links; if the list is empty, the node points to itself and becomes head).

Step 9: Mark the slot as occupied and increase count.

Delete Contact

Step 10: Read contact ID and search the list from head until head is reached again. If not found, display “Contact not found” and stop.

Step 11: If the queue is full, remove the oldest record from the front of the queue.

Step 12: Insert the contact record at the rear of the queue (rear = (rear + 1) % size, queue count = queue count + 1).

Step 13: Free the contact’s slot in the array.

Step 14: Delete the node by linking its previous and next nodes (update head if needed; if it is the only node, head = NULL).

Step 15: Decrease count.

Search Contact

Step 16: Read the contact ID.

Step 17: Traverse the list from head using next until head is reached again.

Step 18: If a node matches, display the contact details; otherwise display “Contact not found”.

Display Contacts

Step 19: Forward: start at head and print each node using next until head is reached again.

Step 20: Backward: start at tail (head->prev) and print each node using prev until tail is reached again.

Undo Last Delete

Step 21: If the queue is empty, display a message and stop.

Step 22: Check the record at the front of the queue. If its ID is already in the list or no slot is free, display a message and stop.

Step 23: Remove the front record from the queue (front = (front + 1) % size, queue count = queue count - 1).

Step 24: Insert the contact at the end of the list and mark a free slot as occupied.

Slot Status and Delete History

Step 25: Slot status: traverse the slot array and print each slot as Free or Occupied.

Step 26: History: print the queue records from front to rear (oldest delete first).

This follows the same layout as your Hospital algorithm and matches your latest code (circular doubly linked list, array and queue). Three things differ from the stack version:

The list is circular, so traversal stops when head is reached again instead of at NULL.
Delete history is FIFO, and undo restores the oldest deleted contact.
Exit is choice = 0.
